docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R64-contract-mcp-schema.md:159:These are test-infrastructure bugs in T58's test fixtures, not contract gate failures. The contract gate (`rpc::mcp_schema_tests`) builds its own `SaveMemoryArgs` payload through the dispatcher and so threads workspace_id automatically; consequently every R64 test passes. The pre-existing R63 reopen of T58 still applies; this gate does not absorb that scope.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R64-contract-mcp-schema.md:176:All files stay under the 800-line file ceiling. The fixture-builder `SchemaFixture::new` is intentionally close to the 50-line function ceiling because it threads every required dependency through `McpHandler::new`; splitting it further would just move the same arguments across helpers and worsen readability. The largest test (39 lines) exceeds the 30-line test guideline because it constructs a full `MemoryRecord` literal — the alternative is a fixture builder, but a builder for one one-shot literal is less clear than the literal itself. The Cadres standard treats the 30-line line as a heuristic; the deviation is justified.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T68.md:6:**Depends on:** T65, T66
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T68.md:12:This task ships the `lattice_report` binary and its supporting `metrics/report.rs` module so operators and CI can render the full Phase 9 metric set from the T65 collector and the T66 benchmark output. Spec §Phase 9 Deliverables names "regression dashboard or CLI report" as binding and the Phase 9 DoD requires that "changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote." The CLI is the surface that turns the metrics module into something a human or CI job can read.
